### Send these with the little green arrow in the IntelliJ gutter.
### Start the app first, and make sure `docker compose up -d` is running.

### 1. Shorten a URL. Copy the shortUrl from the response into request 2.
POST http://localhost:8080/api/shorten
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "longUrl": "https://docs.spring.io/spring-boot/reference/data/sql.html"
}

### 2. Follow a short code. Replace CODE with the one you just got.
### IntelliJ follows redirects by default, so you will see the target page's
### HTML. To see the raw 302 instead, look at the response headers.
GET http://localhost:8080/CODE

### 3. Click stats for that code.
GET http://localhost:8080/api/stats/CODE

### 4. Unknown code -> 404.
GET http://localhost:8080/zzzzzz

### 5. Rejected: not a URL at all -> 400.
POST http://localhost:8080/api/shorten
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "longUrl": "just some text"
}

### 6. Rejected: dangerous scheme -> 400.
### Without the scheme check in ShortlinkService this would be stored and
### later served back inside a redirect from our own domain.
POST http://localhost:8080/api/shorten
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "longUrl": "javascript:alert(1)"
}

### 7. Rejected: blank -> 400, caught by @NotBlank before our code runs.
POST http://localhost:8080/api/shorten
Content-Type: application/json

{
  "longUrl": ""
}

### 8. Is the app alive?
GET http://localhost:8080/actuator/health
